00:05:12.575 --> 00:05:16.695
Whether these venues are available and for which contracts depends on where you live,

00:05:17.215 --> 00:05:20.795
the rules differ by country, province and state and they are changing.

00:05:22.294 --> 00:05:27.454
Who posts a quote and why a venue would pay for one is the mechanism in how it works today.

00:05:28.054 --> 00:05:33.034
A prediction market contract pays one of two amounts when it resolves, so size still open

00:05:33.034 --> 00:05:38.734
at the end jumps to a full payout or to zero. That settlement exposure is a different control problem

00:05:38.734 --> 00:05:43.554
from quoting a stock or a future you can roll. The number on the screen is a probability,

00:05:44.234 --> 00:05:50.034
and the quote is what turns an incoming order into an update of that probability. A paper on archive,

00:05:50.314 --> 00:05:55.914
optimal market making and prediction markets, models that price as a conditional probability generated

00:05:55.914 --> 00:06:01.794
by transforming a latent belief that defuses over time. The market maker picks a bid and an ask

00:06:01.794 --> 00:06:07.494
to maximize expected wealth at the end. Marked to market inventory risk while the market is open,

00:06:07.894 --> 00:06:12.494
and settlement risk on contracts left open at resolution, sit in the same problem.

00:06:13.054 --> 00:06:18.894
The authors derive the Hamilton-Jakoby-Bellman equation and characterize a unique optimal bid and a

00:06:18.894 --> 00:06:24.754
unique optimal ask. Shifting the equation into latent belief space and using a fixed point argument,

00:06:25.134 --> 00:06:30.494
they prove a classical solution exists and is unique, and they verify that the quoting rule from it

00:06:30.494 --> 00:06:36.674
is optimal. Their comparison has no dollar figures in the abstract. A myopic quota maximizes

00:06:36.674 --> 00:06:41.894
only the instantaneous expected mark-to-market profit. The edge on the next trade marked

00:06:41.894 --> 00:06:47.034
at the new probability. The optimal rule also has to care how inventory behaves if the

00:06:47.034 --> 00:06:51.874
latent belief keeps moving and how that inventory pays if the event resolves either way.

00:06:52.594 --> 00:06:56.794
Inventory shifts the quote because a maker already longed the yes side is less willing

00:06:56.794 --> 00:06:59.154
to buy more of it and more willing to sell it.

00:06:59.634 --> 00:07:03.914
Market beliefs shift the quote because the latent probability is the thing being marked.

00:07:04.414 --> 00:07:08.554
Time to resolution shifts the quote because settlement risk dominates when the binary

00:07:08.554 --> 00:07:13.894
payoff is close, and mark to market risk has longer to compound when resolution is far.

00:07:14.714 --> 00:07:19.594
Risk aversion changes how hard those penalties bite, so a more risk averse maker posts

00:07:19.594 --> 00:07:23.614
a wider quote or a sharper inventory skew for the same book.

00:07:23.614 --> 00:07:29.074
Against that myopic benchmark, the paper finds the optimal strategy substantially improves

00:07:29.074 --> 00:07:32.614
downside protection while preserving most expected profit.

00:07:33.054 --> 00:07:36.734
That is about how a quote is chosen once a liquidity setting exists.

00:07:37.134 --> 00:07:40.034
Not a claim that one side of a live contract is cheap.

00:07:40.614 --> 00:07:45.974
The numerical analysis names dependents on inventory, beliefs, time to resolution, and

00:07:45.974 --> 00:07:50.414
risk a version, and it does not report a live exchange test or a fee schedule.

00:07:50.414 --> 00:07:55.854
A second archive paper, Adaptive Liquidity in Prediction Markets via online learning,

00:07:56.274 --> 00:07:59.254
asks how much liquidity the mechanism itself supplies.

00:07:59.854 --> 00:08:05.074
Markets need liquidity to turn trades into informative prices, and ordinary cost-function

00:08:05.074 --> 00:08:07.634
designs fix that liquidity before trading starts.

00:08:08.314 --> 00:08:13.374
The freeze locks a trade-off between price responsiveness and worst-case loss, even though

00:08:13.374 --> 00:08:14.814
order-flow is not stationary.

00:08:15.434 --> 00:08:20.154
A thick book means one trade barely moves the probability, so the price looks calm

00:08:20.154 --> 00:08:25.394
for a mechanical reason. A thin setting means one trade swings it, and the mechanism's

00:08:25.394 --> 00:08:30.214
worst-case loss is the other side of that same design choice. Their construction mixes

00:08:30.214 --> 00:08:35.134
a family of cost-function markets with weights that can be learned, and the mix is still one

00:08:35.134 --> 00:08:41.514
market. The abstract says it preserves no arbitrage, a bounded worst-case loss, expressiveness,

00:08:41.774 --> 00:08:47.014
and a positive upside. Each round, a hybrid structural risk signal scores price impact

00:08:47.014 --> 00:08:52.614
against inventory risk. Standard online learning algorithms then get switching regret guarantees

00:08:52.614 --> 00:08:58.074
against the best sequence of liquidity regimes in hindsight, not merely the single best fixed

00:08:58.074 --> 00:09:03.554
regime. Simulations show the weights shifting when order flow changes and when inventory changes.

00:09:04.074 --> 00:09:09.534
Neither paper names a venue reward schedule or a dollar subsidy paid to quotas. What they do

00:09:09.534 --> 00:09:14.614
establish is why a quote has to be paid for, in the spread or in some other compensation.

00:09:14.614 --> 00:09:19.654
With nobody posting a bid and an ask, a trade has nowhere to land and the probability

00:09:19.654 --> 00:09:20.654
does not update.

00:09:21.234 --> 00:09:23.494
Liquidity set too high, dolls that update.

00:09:23.994 --> 00:09:28.834
Liquidity set too low lets one order rewrite the probability and raises worst case loss.

00:09:29.434 --> 00:09:33.194
A tight spread is a quote someone was willing or compensated to post.

00:09:33.694 --> 00:09:36.414
Not a second independent vote that the probability is right.

00:09:37.034 --> 00:09:40.994
Volume that is a maker trading against incoming orders is flow through a quote.

00:09:41.474 --> 00:09:43.034
Not a pile of separate forecasts.

00:09:43.034 --> 00:09:48.114
costs. A price near resolution is not the same object as a price months out, because

00:09:48.114 --> 00:09:52.474
one paper puts time inside the quoting decision and the other lets the liquidity regimes

00:09:52.474 --> 00:09:57.474
switch as conditions change. Neither result tells you which side of a contract to take.

